    "case_id": "89",
    "case_summary": "A 73-year-old woman with no significant past medical history presented with a palpable lump in her right breast. Physical examination revealed a firm mass in the upper outer quadrant of the right breast, accompanied by skin edema and erythema. There was no noticeable axillary lymphadenopathy. Vital signs, blood and urine analyses, and chest X-ray were all normal. Tumor marker analysis showed a mild elevation in CA-125 (65 U/ml), while CA 19-9, CA 15-3, a-FP, and CEA levels were within normal ranges. Mammography identified a 1.2 cm mass with irregular borders near the axillary process of the right breast, along with two regional lymph nodes measuring 1.5 cm and 2 cm. The American College of Radiology Breast Imaging and Reporting Data Systems (BI-RADS) categorized the findings as high probability of malignancy (category 5). Ultrasonography confirmed the mammographic findings, and thoracic CT scan showed no abnormalities in the lung parenchyma or mediastinum. The patient underwent lumpectomy and sentinel lymph node biopsy.\n\nFrozen section of the specimen revealed granulomatous inflammation. Gross examination of the tumor, which measured 1.8 cm in greatest diameter, revealed the presence of a multinodular, fleshy mass which microscopically consisted of fibrous and lymphoid tissue infiltrated by large epithelioid granulomas with central acellular necrosis and many giant cells. The same morphologic appearance was identified in the sentinel lymph node which measured 0.9 cm in greatest diameter. Special (acid fast) stains failed to demonstrate microorganisms within the necrosis.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
